WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.490
- [Instructor] So let's imagine

00:00:01.490 --> 00:00:03.890
that each of these little circles here,

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:07.910
represent a member of
a population of bugs.

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:11.080
And, we have two different
population of bugs.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:12.780
You could view this as population one

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.720
on the left side of this orange line

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.890
and population two on the
right side of this orange line.

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:20.640
And these bugs, we're gonna think about

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.360
just one trait, their color,
because it's easy to visualize.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:25.670
And let's say that their color

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:29.150
generally helps them
camouflage from predators.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:33.470
So this kind of cloudy, white,
and black, and green layer,

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:35.630
that's maybe the
environment that they're in,

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:36.660
where you might find them.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.840
Maybe it's the surface
of a moss covered tree

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:40.090
or something like that.

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:42.840
And these are two separate populations

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.630
of maybe a related species,
or maybe the same species,

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:47.770
but they're two different populations

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.000
that are not intermixing.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.720
Now the first question is,

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:56.070
which of these these populations
has more genetic diversity?

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:57.340
Well, on the left here,

00:00:57.340 --> 00:00:59.980
we see that they're all the same color.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:01.510
It looks like a pretty useful color.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:03.260
It seems to hide them pretty well,

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:05.030
camouflaged them pretty well.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:07.360
On the right here, it's
clear that there's more

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:08.410
different types of colors.

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:10.070
Some of them are roughly the same color

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:12.110
as what we see on the left hand side,

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:13.810
but some of them are good bit darker

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:15.950
and some of them are good bit lighter.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:16.970
So the one on the right,

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:20.853
the population on the
right has more diversity.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.220
Now, one could argue that some
of these very bright circles

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:26.860
or some of these darker circles,

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:29.040
might be more susceptible to predators,

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.790
and that actually might be the case.

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:34.010
But what we're gonna think
about in this video is not

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:35.840
how many members of the population

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.640
may be more or less
susceptible to predators,

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.140
what we're gonna think about is,

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.650
does the diversity help us

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:45.660
when there might be some type
of changes to the environment?

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:48.500
Does it make the population
as a whole more resilient?

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:49.333
I'm not talking about

00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:51.850
any individual member of the population.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:55.080
And to imagine that, let's imagine

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.270
that there's some type
of environmental change,

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:59.370
maybe there's pollution in the area

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:02.130
that makes the surfaces
that these little bugs

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:05.650
have to live on a good bit darker.

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:08.220
So maybe the surfaces look like this.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:10.030
So now what's likely to happen?

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:13.730
Well, in this reality, all of
the bugs on our left hand side

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:15.880
are now more visible to predators.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:17.500
And so you can imagine the predators

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:19.760
might be able to pick
off a lot more of them.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:20.830
Or in an extreme case,

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:23.200
maybe able to finish
them off all together,

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.550
while on the right some of
the members of the population

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:29.180
are easier to pick off,
like that one, and that one,

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:31.040
and that one, and that one.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:33.190
While some other members of the population

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:35.860
are even more camouflaged
than they were before.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:39.250
Like that one, and that one, and that one.

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:42.830
And so the population
because of its diversity,

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:44.500
yes, a lot of them might die off,

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.120
they might be picked off by predators,

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:49.280
but it looks like there's a
subset of the right population,

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.470
that is gonna be more likely to survive

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:53.960
and then they can continue to reproduce.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:57.730
And so you won't have the
entire population dying off.

00:02:57.730 --> 00:02:59.930
And you could imagine
it the other way around.

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:03.980
Let's say instead of the
environment getting somehow darker,

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:07.820
imagine if the environment
got somehow lighter,

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.250
something like that?

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:11.200
Well, you see a similar phenomenon.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:14.470
All the ones on the left are
now very similarly visible,

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:17.630
and maybe the predators
could pick them off,

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.520
while on the right,
some of them have become

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:20.500
a lot more visible.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:23.210
In this case the darker
ones are now super-visible.

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:25.620
The predators might be
able to pick them off

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:27.800
and even some of the medium colored.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:29.430
But there's a good number of them

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:31.160
that now blend in quite well,

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:33.510
like that one, and that one,

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.430
and I can't even see them that well,

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:37.200
they're blending in so well.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.440
And so once again,

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:40.170
there's certain members of the populations

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:42.660
that because of this more diversity,

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.310
they are more likely to be able to survive

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:48.380
some type of environmental perturbation.

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:50.760
And so since they are
more likely to survive,

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.750
the entire population is
less likely to be killed off.

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:56.260
So the population as a
whole is more resilient.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:58.320
Not saying that every
member of the population

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:01.960
is more resilient, but
the population as a whole.

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:05.000
So the general takeaway here is that

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:10.000
more diversity leads to more
environmental resilience,

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:17.250
or resilience to changes
in the environment.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:19.650
Once again, we're not talking
about every individual member

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:21.210
of the population being more resilient.

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:23.660
We're talking about the
population as a whole

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:26.160
being able to survive in some way,

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:27.823
and then keep carrying on.

